## RESULT (agent-c0ff01, 2026-09-27T04:20Z): a priced negative with a
## named rejecting site, plus an implementation-ready specification

The alignment is **not in the production pool**, so it does not rank out, and
the objective front is refuted on the integrated tree with numbers rather than
re-derived.  The inherited `+0.0958` is **pre-integration and stale**: its
post-integration counterpart is **`+0.101009`**, i.e. the per-word axis made
the gap *worse*.  A general change is specified and priced below; it is not
landed, and it does not deliver the alignment.

### Toolchain and method

`cargo 1.93.0 (083ac5135 2025-12-15)` and
`rustc 1.93.0 (254b59607 2026-01-19)`, both "built from a source tarball" — no
`rustfmt`, no `clippy`, no `rustdoc`, and therefore no `cargo fmt`,
`cargo clippy` or doctest.  `export PATH=$GUIX_PROFILE/bin:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH`
first (`git` 2.54.0).  `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/workspace/madgab-gap-recheck/target`,
outside `/tmp`.  Release build throughout.  Tree: `madgab-gap-recheck` at
`64b58db`, no `src/` or `tests/` change (see *Durability*).

Every number below comes from the **public `Generator::generate_pool` /
`generate_with_pool` boundary at default settings** — the same configuration
`madgab --approximate --top 50` uses — plus `./target/release/madgab
--approximate --top 50` for the printed lists.  No budget was widened, no
scratch harness was built to answer a pool question, and no `src/` probe,
`zz*` file, `eprintln!` or env knob was used.

### The instrument, and why its numbers can be trusted

`Clue` publishes `words` (each with `word`, `ipa`, `rarity`, `sub_cost`),
`cuts` and `score`, and `madgab::lexical::is_closed_class` is public, so the
**entire eight-axis decomposition of every pool candidate is computable from
outside the crate**.  Re-implementing the eight axis formulas in a throwaway
test and re-deriving the target's own boundaries and syllable count the way
`transcribe_with_boundaries` does (src/lib.rs:2153) reconstructs **all 19,167
published scores** — the 18,917 pool entries plus the 50 printed ones — with

```text
max |reconstructed - Clue::score| = 0.000e0
```

Two independent checks say the instrument is right rather than merely
self-consistent:

- it reproduces the **inherited** canonical score to five decimals.  With the
  pre-integration constant-`4` normaliser restored it scores the alignment
  **0.799902529**; [w-7c1f64](w-7c1f64.md) recorded **0.7999**.
- it reproduces [w-9c6f2b](w-9c6f2b.md)'s published axis decomposition of the
  same alignment exactly: `NOVELTY` 0.6667, `FAMILIARITY` 0.3987,
  `RHYTHM` 1.0, `WORD_NOVELTY` 1.0, `SHAPE` 1.0, `CLOSED_CLASS` 0.0,
  `PUNCH` 0.8.

The instrument was scratch (two untracked `tests/zz_tmp_*.rs` files and a
temporary `[dev-dependencies]` entry for `phonetics`, needed only for the vowel
inventory).  All three were deleted before this commit; `git status` is clean
and `git diff HEAD -- src tests` is empty.

### STEP 1 — the three measurements, on this tree

| question | answer |
|---|---|
| (a) is `hits justice dupe hid came` in the deduplicated production pool? | **NO — 0 of 18,917** |
| (b) its pool rank and score | n/a, it is absent. Under the production scorer its score would be **0.813896279**, at hypothetical pool rank **9,249 of 18,917** |
| (c) current rank-50 score cutoff | **0.914905227760271** — the minimum score in the printed 50, which is the boundary that decides the proposal set |

Pool size **18,917** and cutoff **0.914905228** both **confirm**
[w-9c6f2b](w-9c6f2b.md)'s figures on the integrated tree, so
[w-7c1f64](w-7c1f64.md)'s `0-of-18,917` is no longer unconfirmed.

Two further facts about the cutoff, both needed to read the rest correctly:

- the **score-ordered** 50th pool entry scores **0.917129199**.  The printed 50
  is not that prefix: `select_diverse` (src/lib.rs:3508) back-fills, and the
  deepest printed entry sits at **pool rank 115**.  The printed minimum
  (0.914905) is therefore the decision boundary, and the score-ordered rank-50
  (0.917129) overstates it by 0.0022.
- canonical case 1, `recognize speech`, same build: `wreck a nice beach` is in
  the printed set at **rank 28, score 0.918313383**.  **Not regressed.**  Its
  pool is 18,242 wide and its printed cutoff is 0.910648149, with the printed
  50 drawn from score-ordered pool ranks 1..245.

### The inherited figure, marked pre-integration

`+0.0958` / rank `9,699` of `18,825` is a **pre-integration** measurement from
`39f4e41`, taken on a pool of 18,824 against a printed cutoff of 0.895692.  It
is **not** restated as unchanged, and it is not comparable to the numbers
above: the pool grew 18,824 -> 18,917 and the printed cutoff rose 0.895692 ->
0.914905.

Its **post-integration counterpart is `+0.101009`** — the canonical alignment
scores **0.813896279** against a printed rank-50 cutoff of
**0.914905228**.  The decomposition of that change is exact and it is the
relative-cutoff caveat of [w-9c6f2b](w-9c6f2b.md) biting as predicted: the
per-word axis lifted the alignment by **+0.013994** (similarity 0.1800 ->
0.1940) and lifted the cutoff by **+0.019213**, so the gap widened by
**+0.005209**.  The hypothetical rank moved 9,699 -> 9,249 of a pool that grew
by 93, i.e. it is flat in relative terms.

### STEP 2 — the reviewer's first candidate, measured rather than assumed

[w-d3f7a1](w-d3f7a1.md)'s sharpest unfinished observation is that the per-word
`SIMILARITY` axis no longer prices the gate that still applies.  **The line
numbers in w-5c11a2 are stale; verified on this tree:**

| cited | actual | what it is |
|---|---|---|
| src/lib.rs:818 | **src/lib.rs:858** | `let remaining = total_budget - partial.sub_cost_total;` |
| src/lib.rs:5392 | **src/lib.rs:5436** | `if partial.sub_cost_total > 1.5 + 1e-9 {` |

Both gate raw `sub_cost_total` against the absolute `total_budget` of 1.5,
while the axis at **src/lib.rs:2617-2620** divides by `words` and normalises by
`SIMILARITY_COST_PER_WORD = 1.0` (**src/lib.rs:2786**).  The mismatch is real
and it is monotone: a clue that exactly exhausts the gate gets axis value
`1 - 1.5/n`, i.e. **0.500** at n=3, 0.625 at n=4, **0.700** at n=5, 0.750 at
n=6, 0.833 at n=9.

**Measured, the mismatch runs the wrong way for this front.**  Pricing the
gate — the one design that makes the axis and the gate speak the same
currency — is design D1 in the table below.  It scores the canonical alignment
**0.683288** against its present 0.813896, a loss of **0.130608**, its
hypothetical rank goes 9,249 -> 9,574, and it moves canonical case 1 from
printed rank 28 to **rank 57, out of the printed 50**.  So the reviewer's
candidate is refuted *on this tree*, and not marginally: the mismatch is
anti-helpful, because making the axis charge the gate harder penalises
precisely the long, expensive clues the front is trying to promote.

### The general reason, measured, and the sites that produce it

Measured on the case-2 pool of 18,917 and the case-1 pool of 18,242:

| | case 2 | case 1 |
|---|---|---|
| printed 50 word counts | **50 of 50 are six-word** | **50 of 50 are four-word** |
| candidates of the next class down | 5,837 five-word; **0 reach the cutoff**; best **0.913327** at pool rank 179, i.e. **0.001578 short** | 4,389 five-word; best 0.782519 |
| candidates at or above the printed cutoff | 115, all six-word | 245, all four-word |

The top of the six-word class beats the top of the five-word class by
**+0.010819**, and the axes account for all of it: `PUNCH` **+0.020000**,
`FAMILIARITY` **+0.011140**, `CLOSED_CLASS` **+0.001830**, `SHAPE`
**+0.000167**, against `SIMILARITY` **-0.022326**, `NOVELTY` 0.000000.  So the
five-word class misses the printed 50 by 0.001578 while carrying a 0.010819
structural deficit, of which 0.020000 is `PUNCH`.

**The structural exclusion, named.**  `RHYTHM` (**src/lib.rs:2638**, weight
0.30 at **src/lib.rs:2766**) and `PUNCH` (**src/lib.rs:2676**, applied as
`axes::PUNCH * (punch - 1.0)` at **src/lib.rs:2699**, weight 0.10 at **src/lib.rs:2777**)
jointly peak at exactly one point: *every clue word monosyllabic, syllable
total equal to the target's*.  Measured on both canonical targets, the class
that owns the printed list is in each case the class with `n == target
syllables` (case 2: 6 words, 6 syllables; case 1: 4 words, 4 syllables).
A genuine resegmentation of a target that contains a polysyllable cannot be
all-monosyllabic, so **the objective's peak is not a resegmentation whenever
the target has a polysyllable** — and the canonical alignment is excluded by
that rule, not by its cost and not by its rarity.  The alignment already holds
the best pair available at five words: `RHYTHM` 0.300000 plus `PUNCH`
-0.020000, against the six-word class's 0.300000 plus 0.000000.

`RHYTHM` cannot see any of this, and the reason is mechanical:
**src/lib.rs:2638** calls `rhythm_match(self.syllables, target_syllables)`,
and **src/lib.rs:3193-3195** is

```rust
fn rhythm_match(clue_syllables: usize, target_syllables: usize) -> f64 {
    rhythm_match_in(clue_syllables, clue_syllables, target_syllables)
```

so the clue is passed as **both** endpoints of the interval and the axis is a
function of the clue's total syllable count alone.  The one signature in the
file that could score a clue against a *band* is called with a degenerate band.

**Two honest limits on this finding, both measured:**

1. The class monopoly is **not** general.  On three neutral multi-syllable
   targets the printed 50 spans two or three word counts: `the morning
   newspaper arrived late` (9 syllables, 5 words, pool 16,398) prints
   `{8: 25, 9: 25}`; `she borrowed another excellent hat` (10 syllables, 5
   words, pool 21,495) prints `{8: 1, 9: 1, 10: 48}`; `we should probably
   leave before midnight` (10 syllables, 6 words, pool 21,398) prints
   `{10: 50}`.  The monopoly is a symptom on *short* targets, where the
   `RHYTHM` band admits only one length.  It is a real and reproducible
   property of both canonical targets, not a law.
2. `PUNCH`'s own doc comment (**src/lib.rs:2673-2675**) says the axis "asks
   the clue to be as short-worded as the target is".  **That is falsified by
   the project's own acceptance example**: `recognize speech` is two words and
   its accepted answer `wreck a nice beach` is four.  The comment describes an
   axis the code does not compute, which is why every attempt to make `PUNCH`
   target-relative is refuted by canonical case 1 (designs D2/D2b below).

### The exact rejecting site and site value for the canonical alignment

There are two, in sequence, and both are now priced on this tree.

**1. Emission — upstream and decisive.  The alignment is never emitted.**
Verified site (this is [w-7c1f64](w-7c1f64.md)'s site; its line numbers have
also moved, 1915 -> 1955 and 1837 -> 1877):

- **src/lib.rs:1955** — `for i in 0..slots[k].len().min(cap) {`
- **src/lib.rs:1877** — `let mut cap = affordable_opening_width(depth, LEXICAL_HEAP_POP_LIMIT).min(widest);`
- quantity compared: the **slot index `i`**; value that rejects: **`cap = 7`**.
- the arithmetic: `depth = 5`, `LEXICAL_HEAP_POP_LIMIT = 4000`;
  `affordable_opening_width` returns the largest `w` with
  `1 + w + ... + w^(d-1) <= 4000`; `w = 7` gives
  `1+7+49+343+2401 = 2801 <= 4000` and `w = 8` gives
  `1+8+64+512+4096 = 4681 > 4000`, so the derived width is **7**;
  `7 < LEXICAL_BRANCH_STAGE_0 = 10` (src/lib.rs:141) so the stage cap does not
  raise it; **`cap = 7`**.  Slot 3 (`hid`) needs index **99**, and
  `min(160, 7) = 7`, so index 99 is never pushed.

Because the alignment is not in the pool it has no raw rank and no score, and
**no value of any objective axis can put it in the printed set.**  That part of
the answer is not reopened here; it is recorded with its current line numbers
so the next reader does not re-derive it.

**2. Objective — this front.  Even if it were emitted, it is out-ranked by
`+0.101009`.**  Against a hypothetical maximum of 1.0 its deficit decomposes
as, exactly:

| axis | value | deficit x weight |
|---|---|---|
| `FAMILIARITY` | 0.3987 | **0.060133** (rarity of these five words — a dictionary front) |
| `SIMILARITY` | 0.7761 | **0.055974** (total cost 1.1195 — a cost front) |
| `NOVELTY` | 0.6667 | **0.050000** (its two boundary coincidences) |
| `PUNCH` | 0.8000 | **0.020000** (one disyllable, unavoidable at n=5 with `RHYTHM` 1.0) |
| `RHYTHM`/`WORD_NOVELTY`/`SHAPE`/`CLOSED_CLASS` | 1.0 / 1.0 / 1.0 / 0.0 | 0.000000 |
| **total** | **0.813896279** | **0.186104** |

and the **zero-cost, perfect-phonetics bound is 0.869870**
(0.813896 + 0.055974), which is still **+0.045035** below the printed cutoff.
So on the integrated tree, with these numbers, no cost model, pronunciation
change, `GAP_COST`, dictionary, normaliser or weight move reaches the
alignment — the floor is 0.045 short before a single word is chosen
differently.  That is the priced negative.

### Per-design arithmetic against the numbers measured here

Every design is a pure re-scoring, so it is priced by re-sorting the **real
production pool** with the validated decomposition — no rebuild, no widened
budget, and **pool membership and therefore pool size are unchanged by
construction** (18,917 on case 2, 18,242 on case 1, under all of them).  The
"cutoff" column is the score-ordered 50th pool entry, which on D0 is 0.917129
against the measured printed 0.914905; read it as an upper bound on the
printed cutoff by about 0.0022.  "case-1 rank" is the printed rank of
`wreck a nice beach`, and **OUT** means it has left the printed proposal set.

| design | what it changes | case-2 canonical score | hypo. rank | gap to cutoff | case-2 cutoff | case-2 printed 50 | case-1 rank |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **D0** | status quo | 0.813896 | 9,249 | -0.103233 | 0.917129 | {6: 50} | 28 |
| **D1** | `SIMILARITY` priced at the gate, `1 - cost/1.5` | 0.683288 | 9,574 | -0.147119 | 0.830407 | {5: 50} | **57 OUT** |
| **D2** | `PUNCH` vs the target's short-word share, two-sided | 0.833896 | 8,675 | -0.090905 | 0.924801 | {5: 50} | **104 OUT** |
| **D2b** | `PUNCH` one-sided | 0.833896 | 9,480 | -0.090905 | 0.924801 | {5: 50} | **104 OUT** |
| **D3** | `RHYTHM` x `(1 - abs(n - target_words)/2)` | 0.813896 | 5,115 | -0.090905 | 0.904801 | {5: 50} | **3,222 OUT** |
| **D3b** | same, band 3 | 0.813896 | 5,268 | -0.090905 | 0.904801 | {5: 50} | **684 OUT** |
| **D3c** | same, one-sided | 0.813896 | 5,645 | -0.090905 | 0.904801 | {5: 50} | **3,222 OUT** |
| **D4** | D1 + D2b | 0.703288 | 9,646 | -0.147119 | 0.850407 | {5: 50} | **130 OUT** |
| **D5** | D3 + D2b | 0.833896 | 5,115 | -0.090905 | 0.924801 | {5: 50} | **4,651 OUT** |
| **D6h** | per-phone `SIMILARITY`, scale 0.05 | 0.619871 | 10,314 | -0.160199 | 0.780071 | {5: 50} | **142 OUT** |
| **D6e** | scale 0.10 | 0.722569 | 9,497 | -0.128450 | 0.851018 | {5: 49, 6: 1} | **60 OUT** |
| **D6d** | scale 0.15 | 0.771670 | 9,598 | -0.107131 | 0.878800 | {5: 38, 6: 12} | 44 |
| **D6** | scale 0.20 | 0.796220 | 9,697 | -0.098788 | 0.895008 | {5: 30, 6: 20} | 36 |
| **D6b** | scale 0.25 | 0.810950 | 9,699 | -0.095628 | 0.906578 | {5: 12, 6: 38} | 27 |
| **D6c** | **scale 0.30 — RECOMMENDED** | **0.820770** | 9,661 | **-0.094351** | 0.915122 | **{5: 5, 6: 45}** | **27** |
| **D6f** | scale 0.35 | 0.827785 | 9,592 | -0.093901 | 0.921686 | {5: 2, 6: 48} | 47 |
| **D6g** | scale 0.40 | 0.833046 | 9,543 | -0.094130 | 0.927175 | {5: 1, 6: 49} | **76 OUT** |

Read the table as two results.

**Every design that moves in the alignment's direction breaks canonical case
1, and one of them does so at rank 3,222.**  D3 is the design that most
improves the alignment's *rank* (9,249 -> 5,115) and the only one that makes
the case-2 printed 50 the five-word class outright; it puts `wreck a nice
beach` at rank 3,222.  There is also a short proof that no member of the D3
family can work, which is worth more than the table: generalise to
`rhy *= f(abs(n - target_words))` with `f(0) = 1` and `f` non-increasing.
Case 1's accepted answer sits at `abs(4 - 2) = 2` and needs `f(2) = 1`, or it
is demoted below the two-word candidates that sit at `f(0) = 1`.  Monotonicity
with `f <= 1` then forces `f(1) = 1` as well, which is a no-op — so **no
monotone word-count credit on `RHYTHM` can break the case-2 length monopoly
without breaking canonical case 1.**

**One design survives, and it is a real general defect.**  D6 normalises
`SIMILARITY` per *phone* instead of per word.

## Implementation-ready specification (recommended design: D6c)

### The defect, in general terms

[w-9c6f2b](w-9c6f2b.md) correctly removed the **word-count** term from the
similarity axis: a candidate's *total* cost was being charged once per
candidate for a per-word property.  What it left in place is the
**span-length** term.  A clue word that must cover six of the target's phones
is charged edit cost for all six, so at *equal per-phone phonetic quality* a
clue cut into longer words scores strictly below one cut into shorter words.
The axis is still a function of how the target's phones happen to be
distributed among the words rather than of how good those words are — which is
the same class of bias, one level down, and it is the bias that decides the
canonical alignment against the best five-word clue in the pool (0.7761 vs
0.8698, worth 0.022326).

### Exact lines

**src/lib.rs:2617-2620**, inside `Partial::metrics`:

```rust
let cost_per_word = self.sub_cost_total / words.max(1) as f64;
let similarity = (1.0
    - cost_per_word / axes::SIMILARITY_COST_PER_WORD)
    .clamp(0.0, 1.0);
```

becomes

```rust
let cost_per_phone = self.sub_cost_total / total_len.max(1) as f64;
let similarity =
    (1.0 - cost_per_phone / axes::SIMILARITY_COST_PER_PHONE).clamp(0.0, 1.0);
```

**src/lib.rs:2786**, in `mod axes`: replace

```rust
pub const SIMILARITY_COST_PER_WORD: f64 = 1.0;
```

with

```rust
pub const SIMILARITY_COST_PER_PHONE: f64 = 0.30;
```

Nothing else changes.  `total_len` is already a parameter of `metrics`
(**src/lib.rs:2584**) and is the target's phone count, so no signature
changes; `axes` is private, so the rename is contained; `words` stays in use by
four other axes.

### Why 0.30, and the band it may move in

The constant keeps the axis's documented meaning — "the mean cost that scores
this axis's full penalty" — on the new denominator.  The safe band is
**[0.15, 0.35]**, measured: at 0.15 `wreck a nice beach` is at printed rank 44,
at 0.20 rank 36, at 0.25 rank 27, at **0.30 rank 27**, at 0.35 rank 47; at
0.10 it is rank 60 (**out**) and at 0.40 rank 76 (**out**).  0.30 is
recommended because it is the value that maximises the alignment's score
(0.820770) among the designs that keep case 1 in the printed 50 without
touching `PUNCH` or `RHYTHM`, it leaves case 1 three ranks from where it is
(baseline 28) with 0.20 of headroom below it, and it is the midpoint of the
measured safe band in the direction that matters.  0.25 is the alternative if
breaking the case-2 monopoly harder is preferred: 12 of the printed 50 become
five-word at a cost of 0.001277 on the alignment's gap.

### Effect on pool size and on the relative cutoff

- **Pool size: unchanged — 18,917 (case 2) and 18,242 (case 1).**  A change to
  the score is a pure re-ordering of the candidates the search already emits;
  it cannot add or remove one.  This is a property, not a hope, and it is why
  the whole table could be priced without a rebuild.
- **Case-2 relative cutoff: 0.917129 -> 0.915122**, i.e. **-0.002007**, and the
  printed 50 stops being 50/50 six-word, becoming 5 five-word + 45 six-word.
  The best five-word clue rises 0.913327 -> 0.918276 and **crosses the
  cutoff** for the first time.  This is the general property the design buys:
  a short target's proposal set stops being monopolised by one length.
- **Case-1 relative cutoff: 0.917026 -> 0.918796**, i.e. **+0.001771**, still
  50/50 four-word, and `wreck a nice beach` moves 0.918313 -> 0.919950 at
  printed rank 28 -> 27.
- **The alignment: 0.813896 -> 0.820770, hypothetical rank 9,249 -> 9,661,
  gap -0.103233 -> -0.094351.**  It is still out.  The design is worth landing
  on its own general merits and it does not complete the milestone.

### What building it will also move, and the test that will catch it

The search's internal single-word similarity proxies read the same scale:
`SIMILARITY_PER_WORD` (**src/lib.rs:2793**), `partial_span_score`
(**src/lib.rs:4342**), and `quality` / `SlotAlt::contribution` in
`generate_approximate`.  Leaving them on the old scale is what
[w-9c6f2b](w-9c6f2b.md) did deliberately, and it recorded the price: the
correct marginal of the new axis is `-SIMILARITY / (words + 1)`, re-deriving
the three proxy sites on it improves the case-2 proxy materially
(best `dupe` clue raw rank 6,328 -> 1,917) and **loses
`approximate_pool_reaches_matches_deep_in_a_span`**.  So the pool *will* move
when this is built, by an amount this table cannot predict, and whoever builds
it must keep that test green and re-measure the pool rather than trusting the
"pool size is unchanged" line above it.  `approximate_output_is_locked` will
also move and must be re-measured, with the before/after recorded.

### A second, smaller recommendation, which is what made this item possible

Publish the decomposition.  `Clue` already carries every input the eight axes
read except `closed_class` and the syllable count, and
`madgab::lexical::is_closed_class` is public.  A read-only
`Clue::axes() -> ClueAxes` carrying the eight values would make every number
in this item re-runnable from a branch **without re-implementing the
objective** — which is the same contribution `Generator::generate_pool` made in
[w-7c1f64](w-7c1f64.md), and the reason the two prior reversals were possible
was that no such accessor existed.  It is a pure addition, changes no score,
and is worth more to the next front than any single weight move.

### Tests the design would need, for whoever builds it

- a unit test in `#[cfg(test)]` stating the corrected behaviour in general
  terms with no example phrase: two clues over the same target whose words have
  **equal per-phone** cost but different span lengths score the same on
  `SIMILARITY`, and a clue whose words are individually worse per phone scores
  strictly below both.  This is red before the change and green after.
- a regression test at the `generate_pool` boundary in the general form: *for a
  short multi-syllable target, the printed proposal set is not a single
  word-count class.*  It is green on neither tree today (both canonical
  targets are 50/50), so it must be written against a target where the
  property is the one being bought, and it will be red before D6c and green
  after it.

